BARK Indexing
=============

BARK is an index access method designed as a successor to nbtree (see the
top-level BARK-Design.mediawiki for the full contract catalog and roadmap).
This directory holds the implementation; this README describes the on-disk
format defined in src/include/access/bark.h.  The reading and writing code
arrives in later commits -- at this point the format is defined but no index
can yet be built.

Page structure
--------------

BARK borrows nbtree's Lehman & Yao page structure so the high-concurrency
search and split algorithm can be shared.  Every page is a standard
PostgreSQL page whose special space holds a BarkPageOpaqueData:

    bark_prev / bark_next   right-linked sibling chain (BARK_P_NONE at edges)
    bark_level              0 for leaf pages, increasing toward the root
                            (16 bits)
    bark_cycleid            vacuum cycle ID of the latest leaf split, or 0
                            (16 bits; see "VACUUM" below)
    bark_flags              page role: BARK_LEAF, BARK_ROOT, BARK_META,
                            BARK_DELETED, BARK_HALF_DEAD,
                            BARK_INCOMPLETE_SPLIT, BARK_HAS_GARBAGE,
                            BARK_OVERFLOW, BARK_PREFIX
    bark_page_id            BARK_PAGE_ID (0xFF84), so external tools can
                            tell BARK pages from nbtree pages: nbtree keeps
                            its vacuum cycle ID in the last two bytes of the
                            special space, and those never exceed
                            MAX_BT_CYCLE_ID (0xFF7F); hash, GiST, SP-GiST and
                            bloom use 0xFF80-0xFF83

As in nbtree, each page carries a high key (an upper bound on the keys the
page may hold) and a right-link to its sibling, which together let a scan
detect and recover from a concurrent split without holding read locks across
pages.

Leaf prefix compression
-----------------------

With the prefix_compression reloption (default off), a leaf whose index has a
varlena first key column may be flagged BARK_PREFIX.  Such a leaf holds a
PREFIX item right after its high key (at BarkPagePrefixOff; the data items
start one offset later, at BarkPageFirstDataKey): an IndexTupleData header,
with t_tid zero and t_info set to the item's size, then 1..126 bytes of
prefix.  Each SINGLE, LIST and POSTING entry with a non-NULL first column
stores that column coded: a varlena whose payload's first byte gives how
many bytes of the prefix the value starts with (0..126), the rest being the
value's remaining bytes; the value's varlena header is recomputed on decode
(1-byte when it fits, else 4-byte).  A value compressed inline, or with a
header not in that form, is stored raw (count 127, then the value with its
header).  The bytes after the first column are moved to keep their
alignment, or left out when they are only the key's closing zero padding.
The format comment at BARK_PREFIX in bark.h has the details.  OVERSIZED
entries, NULL first columns, high keys and pivots are stored as on any page.

Every read of a data item goes through BarkPageGetItem, which decodes on a
BARK_PREFIX page, so comparisons always see the real datum and any collation
works.  Coding needs only the page's prefix and the entry, not the catalog,
so WAL redo can repeat it (ADD_TID redo decodes, adds the TID, and codes
again); the other records carry coded bytes and redo adds them as they are.

The prefix is chosen only when a leaf is written whole, and never changes:
the build gives a leaf its first item's leading bytes when the previous
leaf's items shared at least 4 of its own on average (the first leaf always
gets one), and each half of a leaf split takes its first item's bytes, cut
to the longest share, when its items share at least 4 bytes on average.
Because each entry records its own share, an entry that shares less (an
insert outside the page's original range, say) is still stored correctly,
so no insert, VACUUM or change of the key range ever forces a page rewrite.
A coded entry can be up to 8 bytes larger than the plain one (the count
byte crossing an alignment boundary), so every free-space test before
placing an entry on a leaf uses bark_coded_size.  A split sizes items as
they are coded on the original page; a half that does not fit with its new
prefix is laid out plain, or, if that does not fit either, with the original
page's prefix, against which it fits by construction.  The split record
says which halves have a prefix (XLH_BARK_SPLIT_LPREFIX / _RPREFIX).

Page splits and the incomplete-split flag
-----------------------------------------

A split is two WAL records, as in nbtree.  The first rebuilds the left page,
fills the new right page, and links it in; the left page is flagged
BARK_INCOMPLETE_SPLIT.  The second inserts the right page's downlink into the
parent and clears the flag.  The splitting backend keeps the left page
write-locked from before the first record until after the second, releasing
it only once it holds the parent's write lock and has written the downlink
(the right page is released as soon as the first record is written).  Locks
are taken child before parent at every level.

So a backend that sees BARK_INCOMPLETE_SPLIT under a page lock is looking at a
split nobody is completing: an error or crash interrupted it.  An insert's
descent finishes every such split it reaches, at any level, before it moves
right past it or descends from it (bark_finish_split, with the same locking),
as nbtree's _bt_moveright does.  Merely moving right would not be enough: the
right half has no downlink, so inserts that only ever land on the right half
would split it with no parent entry to insert after.  Every descent, for read
or write, also moves right past pages flagged BARK_DELETED or BARK_HALF_DEAD,
which are never the page a key belongs on.

Inserting a downlink finds the parent by matching the child's block number in
the parent level, starting at the descent's stack entry and moving right,
rather than by key (bark_getstackbuf, after _bt_getstackbuf).  The new
downlink goes immediately after the one to its left sibling.  A split of a
root page that no longer is the root (another backend added a level) finds
its parent from the leftmost page of the next level up.

An empty index (one whose meta page names no root, which only an unlogged
index reset after a crash can be) gets an empty root leaf under the meta
page's exclusive lock; the insert then descends again and goes through the
normal checks, so two backends racing to insert the first key cannot both
skip the uniqueness check.

The split point is chosen as nbtree's nbtsplitloc.c chooses it
(bark_findsplitloc, barksplitloc.c), by bytes rather than item count: the
rightmost page on a level leaves its left half at the fillfactor (the leaf
fillfactor reloption, or BARK_NONLEAF_FILLFACTOR), so ascending inserts fill
pages the way CREATE INDEX does; other pages split evenly.  On a leaf the
choice among nearby split points prefers one between keys that differ early,
moves out of a run of equal keys when the page is not all one run, and leaves
the left page BARK_SINGLEVAL_FILLFACTOR full when the page holds a single key
that no later page holds.  Equal keys may sit on both sides of a split; the
high key between them then carries a heap TID (see "Heap TID order").

A split point falls between entries, and a LIST or POSTING entry can take a
third of the page, so on such a page the single-value split would leave the
left page with every entry that fits, nearly full.  When the incoming heap
TID sorts after every entry on it, as inserts in heap TID order do, the last
entry is cut instead (bark_singleval_cut, bark_entry_cut): its lower part
stays, sized so the left page ends at the fill factor, and its upper part,
with the new TID, goes to the right page.  The split's record logs both
whole.  nbtree reaches the same fill by capping the last posting list its
deduplication pass forms on such a page (_bt_singleval_fillfactor).  The
free space matters after VACUUM: rows inserted into the heap space it frees
get heap TIDs throughout each key's run, and go back to these pages; pages
left nearly full would mostly overflow and split evenly.

Suffix truncation
-----------------

A pivot (a page's high key, or the downlink to a page) keeps only as many
leading key attributes as it needs, as in nbtree (_bt_truncate).  When a leaf
splits between lastleft, the left page's last item, and firstright, the right
page's first item, the left page's new high key is firstright with every key
attribute after the first one on which the two differ removed: it keeps
1 + (the number of leading key attributes on which lastleft and firstright
are equal), up to all of them (bark_truncate_pivot, bark_keep_natts).  When
the two are equal on every key attribute, the pivot keeps them all and a heap
TID, firstright's lowest, as nbtree's does (BARK_PIVOT_HEAP_TID; see "Heap
TID order").  INCLUDE columns are never kept.  Equality is decided by the
opclass comparator, not by comparing stored images: two values can differ
in image yet compare equal, and what matters is how the pivot compares.  The
downlink inserted into the parent is a copy of the high key, so the parent
separates the two pages by the same tuple.  An internal page's items are pivots already, so its split
copies the right page's first item, with the attributes it has, as the new
high key.  CREATE INDEX does the same: each finished leaf's high key is
truncated against its last item, and a copy of that high key is the next
page's downlink (nbtsort.c's _bt_buildadd).  A single-column index never
truncates.

The removed attributes compare as minus infinity (bark_compare_itups orders a
pivot with fewer attributes before a key that agrees on the attributes it
has, which also makes a level's first downlink, with none, minus infinity).
So if T is firstright truncated, lastleft < T <= firstright, and T equals
firstright only when nothing was removed.  BARK's invariant, keys(left page)
<= high key <= keys(right page), still holds, and so does
keys(child i-1) <= downlink i <= keys(child i) for every downlink; with heap
TIDs, (key, TID) of every left entry < high key <= (key, lowest TID) of every
right entry.  The consequences:

  * A lower-bound descent (nextkey=false, scans, and the unique check)
    compares keys only, follows the last downlink less than its bound, and
    so never starts right of the first match.
  * An insert descends with its key and heap TID straight to the leaf the
    entry belongs on (see "Heap TID order").
  * A key that is greater than T but less than firstright (it shares T's
    attributes and sorts below firstright) now belongs to the right page, for
    inserts and searches alike.  No such key existed when the split was made,
    since lastleft and firstright were adjacent.
  * A lower bound equal to a pivot that has exactly the bound's columns and
    no heap TID sorts after the pivot (bark_compare_bound), as a forward
    scankey does in nbtree's _bt_compare: every entry left of the pivot is
    strictly less than it on those columns, so the descent goes right of
    it rather than reading a leaf that holds no match.  A pivot with more
    columns than the bound, or with a heap TID, may have entries equal to
    the bound on its left, and the bound still sorts before it.

The split-point choice (barksplitloc.c) prefers split points whose neighbors
differ on an earlier attribute, so the high key keeps fewer attributes.  As
in nbtree, it sizes the new high key as if nothing were truncated.

An OVERSIZED pivot stays OVERSIZED when one of the attributes it keeps is too
large to fit inline; its BarkOverflowRef.natts records the attributes kept.
A high key and the downlink copied from it share one overflow chain, as do an
internal page's high key and the item it is copied from.  Pivot chains are
never freed (VACUUM frees only the chains of leaf entries it removes), so the
sharing needs no reference count.

Heap TID order
--------------

Leaf entries are ordered by (key, heap TID), as nbtree's are since PG12
(version 4 of its format).  Every leaf entry covers a range of heap TIDs
(bark_entry_tid_range): a SINGLE its t_tid, an OVERSIZED entry its ref's
locator, a LIST its first to last member, a POSTING its sbm's minimum to
maximum.  Within a run of equal keys the ranges are disjoint and ascending,
across pages too.  Nothing else is ordered by TID: scans, VACUUM, the KNN
search, split-point choice and the build's grouping of runs compare keys
only (bark_compare_itups); only placement uses bark_compare_itups_tid.

A pivot between two entries equal on every key attribute keeps the right
entry's lowest TID after its key data, as the last ItemPointerData of the
MAXALIGNed tuple, with BARK_PIVOT_HEAP_TID set in its status nibble (the bit
BARK_IS_DELETE_MARKED has on a leaf entry, which a pivot never has); an
OVERSIZED pivot keeps it in BarkOverflowRef.pivottid.  A pivot without one
is minus infinity on the TID.  To leave room for it, a leaf entry is stored
inline only up to BarkMaxItemSize less 8 bytes, as nbtree's BTMaxItemSize
does, and a split sizes a leaf's new high key with it.

An insert descends with (key, TID), moving right past a high key whose
(key, TID) is <= its own (an equal TID belongs right, since the high key's
is the right page's lowest), and goes in front of the first entry that sorts
after it.  A unique check (nbtree's _bt_doinsert) descends on the key alone
to the first leaf that can hold it, since the live entry of the key may be
anywhere in the run; bark_check_unique scans right from there while keys
are equal; the insert then steps right, with lock coupling, to the leaf the
TID belongs on (bark_insert_stepright, _bt_stepright).

As in nbtree's _bt_search_insert, an insert that placed its entry on the
rightmost leaf of a tree whose root is at level 2 or higher caches that leaf
(RelationSetTargetBlock), or after splitting it the new right page.  The next
insert without a uniqueness check, of a key that is not oversized, takes the
cached leaf instead of descending if a conditional exclusive lock on it
succeeds and it is still a live rightmost leaf with no unfinished split,
holding at least one entry, with (key, TID) sorting after the first and room
for the new entry (bark_fastpath_leaf).  Such an insert has no stack and so
must not split: an insert whose TID falls inside an entry that cannot take it
gives the leaf up and descends.  A unique check never takes the fast path,
since a run of the key may begin on the cached leaf's left sibling.  An
insert that lands anywhere else forgets the cached leaf.

Coalescing considers the entry just before the insert position, whose range
starts at or below the new TID.  A TID above its range is added to it (the
common case) or gets a SINGLE of its own.  A TID inside its range must go
into it.  When the entry cannot take it, the entry is divided around the TID
into two entries of the key (bark_entry_swap_tid): in the middle of its
members, not nbtree's posting-list swap of the highest member, because BARK
has no deduplication pass to merge the SINGLEs such swaps would leave.  The
two parts replace the entry in place (XLOG_BARK_INSERT_SWAP) when they fit,
else in a page split (bark_split's replaced entry, nbtree's origpostingoff).
A TID already in the entry is reported as corruption, as nbtree does.

A leaf insert keeps 8 bytes of a page's free space back (bark_leaf_free_space).
Coalescing fills a page a few bytes at a time, to the last byte; when such a
page splits inside a run, its new high key, which has a heap TID, is 8 bytes
larger than the one it replaces, and without the reserve the left half could
not keep all its entries: a LIST of up to a third of a page would move right
for want of 8 bytes, leaving that much free on the left for good.

Indexes built before heap TID order (version 1) are refused with a hint to
REINDEX them: the meta page's version is checked in bark_get_root, which
every descent reaches on a root-cache miss, and by bark_index_check.

Block 0 is always the meta page (BarkMetaPageData): a magic number
(BARK_MAGIC), the on-disk version (BARK_VERSION), the current root block and
its level, and whether all key columns are "equalimage" (which governs
whether duplicate keys can be deduplicated losslessly).  A descent does not
read it: the root block and level are cached in rd_amcache, as nbtree caches
its meta page, and the cached page is used only if, under its lock, it is
still a root (BARK_ROOT set, not being deleted) at the cached level.  A root
split clears BARK_ROOT on the old root, so a stale cache entry fails that
check and the meta page is read instead.

Every read of a data item (any item but a high key) goes through
BarkPageGetItem, which decodes the prefix-coded items of a BARK_PREFIX leaf
(see "Leaf prefix compression").

VACUUM
------

barkbulkdelete reads the index in physical block order, as btbulkdelete
does, and deletes the entries whose heap TIDs are dead.  Only live leaf pages
are cleaned; deleted pages, internal pages and overflow pages are skipped.

A leaf split during the scan can move entries from a page the scan has not
reached yet to a right page at a block it has already passed (a split takes
its right page from the free space map, which can hand out any block).  To
catch that, BARK uses nbtree's vacuum cycle IDs, and nbtree's registry of
them (_bt_start_vacuum, _bt_vacuum_cycleid, _bt_end_vacuum), which keys on
the relation's LockRelId and is not specific to nbtree.  A leaf split stamps
both halves with the cycle ID of the VACUUM running on the index, if any,
while it holds both pages locked.  When VACUUM cleans a page that carries its
cycle ID and whose right sibling is at a lower block than the scan position,
it follows the right link and cleans that page too, and so on while the pages
it reaches carry its cycle ID.  It clears the cycle ID from each such page it
cleans, so the page is not cleaned a second time.  The clear is WAL-logged,
in the page's XLOG_BARK_VACUUM record (see "WAL" below), where nbtree treats
it as a hint.

VACUUM takes a cleanup lock on every live leaf, whether or not it has
entries to delete, as nbtree does (see "Deleting index tuples during VACUUM"
in the nbtree README).  An index-only scan, and any scan with a non-MVCC
snapshot, keeps a pin on the leaf it is returning entries from, so VACUUM
cannot delete a TID the scan has read but not yet returned, and the heap
line pointer the TID refers to cannot be reused while the scan still needs
it.  A plain index scan with an MVCC snapshot drops the pin once it has read
the page (see "Scans" below), as nbtree's does: its snapshot rejects a
recycled line pointer's new tuple, so an idle cursor does not hold up
VACUUM.  Replay of VACUUM's record takes a cleanup lock too,
as nbtree's does, so the interlock also holds for scans on a hot standby:
replay waits for a standby scan's pin, or cancels the scan once
max_standby_streaming_delay has passed.

Bottom-up deletion
------------------

As in nbtree (see "Bottom-Up deletion" in the nbtree README), an insert
that the executor marks indexUnchanged -- a new version from a non-HOT
UPDATE that did not change this index's key -- and that would split a leaf
first runs bark_bottomup_delete (barkdelete.c) on the leaf.  Every heap TID
on the page goes to table_index_delete_tuples with bottomup set; the TIDs of
entries whose key equals the new entry's, and every LIST and POSTING member,
are marked promising.  The table AM visits a few heap blocks and reports the
TIDs whose HOT chains are dead to every snapshot.  Their entries are deleted,
and a LIST or POSTING that keeps some members is re-formed with them, by the
same code VACUUM uses (bark_reform_entry), in one XLOG_BARK_DELETE record.
If that left room for the new entry it is inserted; otherwise the leaf splits
as before.  Coalescing into an equal-key LIST or POSTING still comes first.

OVERSIZED entries are not offered to the table AM: deleting one has to free
its overflow chain once the leaf no longer references it, which VACUUM does.

The deletion runs under the exclusive lock the insert holds, not a cleanup
lock, and its redo takes an exclusive lock too, as nbtree's simple and
bottom-up deletion do (see "Simple deletion" there).  The heap tuples are
dead to every snapshot, and the heap does not reuse their line pointers
until VACUUM, which takes a cleanup lock on every leaf first; so a scan that
copied one of the deleted TIDs before the deletion only finds a dead tuple
at it.  On a hot standby, redo first cancels the queries whose snapshots
could still see those tuples (the record's snapshotConflictHorizon).  No
entry moves to another page, so VACUUM's cycle IDs are unaffected; a leaf
the pass empties is an empty leaf, which VACUUM deletes.

Merging a page's entries before a split
---------------------------------------

As nbtree runs its deduplication pass (_bt_dedup_pass) before splitting a
leaf, an insert into an index whose entries coalesce (see
bark_allequalimage) that would split a leaf first runs bark_merge_page
(barkdelete.c) on it: runs of adjacent entries of one key are merged, in
page order, into fewer, larger LIST or POSTING entries, each up to
BarkMaxItemSize, in the shape bark_form_entry chooses by size.  Entries of
a key are in heap TID order with disjoint ranges, so a merged entry's
members are already ascending.  SINGLE entries fold in, OVERSIZED ones are
skipped, and a merge never spans the new entry's insert offset.  The pass
stops once the page has room for the new entry; if it made room, the entry
is inserted, else the leaf splits as before (the merge stays).  The two
places it runs are the ones where an insert would otherwise split: an entry
that does not fit (after bottom-up deletion, if that ran), and a LIST or
POSTING that a new heap TID falls inside but that can neither take it nor be
divided in place (bark_coalesce_list's BARK_COALESCE_SPLIT), which is what a
reinsert into a CREATE INDEX-built index meets, its LISTs covering the whole
TID range of each page.  After the merge the insert offset is found again,
and the coalesce is tried once more.  The single-value split's cut
(bark_singleval_cut) is left alone: it means to split.

Each group's first entry is overwritten with the merged entry and the rest
are deleted, in one XLOG_BARK_MERGE record.  No heap TID leaves the page and
no entry moves to another page, so the pass needs only the exclusive lock
the insert holds, as nbtree's does: scans copy the items they return under
the page's share lock and reposition by key and heap TID, never by offset,
and VACUUM's cycle IDs are unaffected.  Redo takes an exclusive lock too and
has no recovery conflict to resolve.

Scans
-----

A scan reads a leaf page at a time, as nbtree's _bt_readpage does
(bark_readpage): under the page's share lock it copies every matching heap
TID into the scan position (BarkScanPosData), and for an index-only scan
each matching entry's key into a tuple workspace, then saves the sibling
links and lets the lock go.  The executor is handed items from that copy, so
concurrent inserts and splits on the page cannot make the scan return an
entry twice or skip one.  Moving to the next page follows the saved right
link; moving left goes through bark_lock_and_validate_left, which recovers
from a split or deletion of the left sibling as nbtree's
_bt_lock_and_validate_left does.  A plain MVCC scan drops its pin after the
read (nbtree's dropPin rule); index-only scans and non-MVCC scans keep it.
A parallel scan hands pages out through a shared cursor with nbtree's
seize/release protocol.  Mark and restore copy the position, as nbtree's do;
the executor does not restore a scan with ORDER BY operators, since its
reorder queue would not be rewound.

The first descent uses the longest bound the keys give on the leading
columns (bark_make_bound): equality on columns 1..k-1 and a bound on column
k, compared through the opfamily's cross-type ORDER procs.  A lower bound
sorts before every key equal to it and an upper bound after, so a backward
scan descends straight to its last match.  A row comparison bounds the
descent with its usable prefix, the members on consecutive columns from its
first one that are not NULL and keep the first member's direction, as
nbtree's _bt_first does, and one on column 1 also ends the scan once an
entry sorts past that prefix.  A leading-column ScalarArrayOp
re-descends to its next element when that element lies past the next page.

Skip scan.  With no key on column 1 and a key that bounds column 2, the
scan skips the parts of each column-1 group that cannot match: on a page,
by binary search to the next possible match (bark_skip_on_page); across
pages, by re-descending when the next possible match sorts after the page's
high key (bark_skip_plan).  When column 1's opclass has skip support (a
discrete type such as int4 or date), the scan goes from a group past column
2's bounds straight to (next value, column 2's lower bound); otherwise it
descends past the whole group first.  It skips forward only, and only over
column 1; nbtree also skips over several leading columns.

On every leaf after a descent's first, bark_readpage first tests the scan
keys against the page's first and last data entries, as nbtree's
_bt_set_startikey does (bark_page_satisfied_keys).  A key on column k that
both satisfy, where the two are equal on columns 1..k-1, holds for every
entry between them, so it is not tested again on that page.  Arrays and
row comparisons are always tested.  The first leaf a descent reaches skips
the check, as in nbtree: a selective lookup reads only that page and would
pay for the check without gaining from it.

Ordered-operator (KNN) scans, ORDER BY col <~> const, walk outward from
the constant in two cursors, one in each direction, each reading a page at
a time like the plain scan, and merge them by distance (barkknn.c).  They
are planned only on an index's leading column, with one ordering operator;
an ordered index keeps a later column's values spread over the whole index.
A scroll cursor over a KNN scan is materialized: the scan cannot run its
distance order backward.

Page deletion and recycling
---------------------------

VACUUM reads the index once, in physical order, as nbtree's btvacuumscan
does (bark_vacuum_scan): the same pass that deletes dead entries counts the
entries left and the deleted pages, records in the FSM the deleted pages that
are safe to reuse, and notes each leaf it leaves empty.  barkvacuumcleanup
does nothing after a bulk delete; when no bulk delete ran (no dead tuples
this cycle) it runs the same pass without deleting entries.  So VACUUM's
cleanup is cheap after a bulk delete, as nbtree's is, and BARK declares
VACUUM_OPTION_PARALLEL_COND_CLEANUP as nbtree does.

At the end of the pass, VACUUM deletes the empty interior leaves it noted
(bark_delete_empty_leaf): in one XLOG_BARK_UNLINK_PAGE record it
splices the leaf out of the sibling chain, points the leaf's downlink in the
parent at the right sibling and removes the right sibling's old downlink (so
the leaf's key space moves right, as in nbtree), and turns the leaf into a
deleted page.
VACUUM also deletes the overflow pages of the OVERSIZED entries it removes
(bark_free_oversized).

A deleted page has no items (pd_lower = SizeOfPageHeaderData) and holds a
BarkDeletedPageData at the end of the tuple area (pd_upper = pd_special -
MAXALIGN(sizeof(BarkDeletedPageData))); BARK_DELETED is its only flag, and
its level and cycle ID are zero.  BarkPageSetDeleted reinitializes the page
in this form from its links and safexid alone, so that VACUUM and WAL redo
build the same page.
BarkDeletedPageData holds safexid, the next full transaction ID read while
the page and its neighbours were locked for the deletion.  nbtree puts its
BTDeletedPageData at the start of the tuple area under pd_lower; BARK puts it
at the end so that a reader which does not test the flag sees an empty page.

The page keeps the sibling links it had (an overflow page keeps its link to
the next chunk).  A descent that read the parent's downlink, or a scan that
read a sibling link, before the deletion can arrive at the page afterwards;
it moves right through it, as nbtree's readers move through P_IGNORE pages.
Its neighbours no longer link back to it, which amcheck allows.

A deleted page is reused only when BarkPageIsRecyclable says so, which is
nbtree's BTPageIsRecyclable test: GlobalVisCheckRemovableFullXid(heaprel,
safexid).  Any backend that might hold a link to the page took its snapshot
before the deletion, so its xmin is no later than safexid; once safexid is
removable by that horizon, no such backend remains.  VACUUM keeps the leaves
it deletes in a pending list and records in the FSM, at its end, those whose
safexid is already removable (nbtree's _bt_pendingfsm_finalize).  The cleanup
pass's page walk records every other deleted page in the FSM once it becomes
recyclable, so a page not recyclable at one VACUUM is recorded by a later
one; it also counts the deleted pages (pages_deleted).  bark_get_free_page
repeats the test before it reuses an FSM page, since the FSM is not crash-
safe.  Every path that allocates a page passes the index's heap relation for
the test: inserts and VACUUM directly, and the paths reached from a descent
(bark_finish_split, the overflow chains of OVERSIZED pivots) through
BarkKeyInfo.heaprel, which the insert or VACUUM that started the descent set.

A page reused from the FSM is reused on a hot standby too, by replay of the
record that reinitializes it, and a standby query may still hold a link to
it: the standby's snapshots are not part of the primary's horizon unless
hot_standby_feedback is on.  So before reusing a deleted page,
bark_get_free_page writes an XLOG_BARK_REUSE_PAGE record carrying the page's
safexid, as nbtree's _bt_allocbuf does, whenever wal_level allows hot standby.
Its replay cancels every standby query whose snapshot's xmin is not after
safexid (ResolveRecoveryConflictWithSnapshotFullXid), which is the standby's
version of the GlobalVisCheckRemovableFullXid test the primary made.

safexid is ReadNextFullTransactionId() rather than an assigned XID, as in
nbtree, so a deleted page becomes recyclable only after some transaction that
starts after the deletion takes an XID and ends: on an idle system, pages
deleted by one VACUUM are recorded by the next one after the next write.

WAL
---

BARK has its own WAL resource manager, "Bark" (RM_BARK_ID; barkxlog.h,
barkxlog.c, rmgrdesc/barkdesc.c), for inserts, splits, new roots,
overflow chains, VACUUM, bottom-up deletion, merges and page deletion; CREATE INDEX logs the pages it
writes whole.  Each change to a page is in exactly one record.  The
records:

  XLOG_BARK_INSERT_LEAF (bark_insert_entry, from bark_insert)
      One leaf: the new entry and its offset, as nbtree's INSERT_LEAF.  An
      OVERSIZED entry's overflow chain is written first, in its own
      XLOG_BARK_OVERFLOW records.  Redo adds the entry with PageAddItem under an exclusive lock.

  XLOG_BARK_INSERT_UPPER (bark_insert_entry, from bark_insert_parent)
      Two pages: the parent, with the downlink to the right half of a split
      and its offset, and the left half, whose BARK_INCOMPLETE_SPLIT flag is
      cleared, as nbtree's INSERT_UPPER.  The primary holds both locks;
      redo, like btree_xlog_insert, clears the flag first and then adds the
      downlink, one page lock at a time, since only writers look at the flag.
      A parent that must split to take the downlink is logged as a split.

  XLOG_BARK_SPLIT (bark_split)
      Up to four pages: the left half (the original block), the new right
      half, the original right sibling, whose left link now names the right
      half, and, for an internal page taking a child's downlink, that child,
      whose BARK_INCOMPLETE_SPLIT flag is cleared.  The record holds the
      level, a leaf flag, the cycle ID stamped on both halves and the
      original page's two sibling links.  As in nbtree, the right half is
      logged whole and the left half is not.  The right half's data is its
      tuple area (pd_upper to pd_special), which the primary builds by
      adding the entries in offset order to an empty temporary page, and
      redo re-adds them the same way (bark_restore_page, as nbtree's
      _bt_restore_page), so every entry lands at the offset and position it
      has on the primary.  The left half is rebuilt from the original page,
      which redo has (it is the same block), as btree_xlog_split does: the
      record carries the offset of the first entry that moved right, the new
      high key, the new entry if it went left, and the replacement of a
      divided or cut entry if that stayed left, both coded for the original
      page; primary and redo both build the half with bark_split_build_left,
      which copies the original page's entries as they are stored, PREFIX
      item included.  A leaf split whose left half takes a prefix other than
      the original page's (see "Leaf prefix compression") re-codes every
      entry, so that half is then logged whole, like the right half, and a
      record flag says so.  The left block is registered as a standard
      page, so the first split after a checkpoint carries its full-page
      image instead.  Redo clears the child's flag first, then holds the
      right half, the left half and the right sibling locked together, as
      btree_xlog_split does.

  XLOG_BARK_NEWROOT (bark_new_root)
      Three pages: the new root, rebuilt from its two downlinks as a split
      half is; the old root, the left child, whose split the new root
      finishes; and the meta page, which gets the new root and level.

  XLOG_BARK_CREATE_ROOT (bark_create_root_leaf)
      Two pages: the first root of an empty index, an empty leaf, and the
      meta page.  CREATE INDEX always writes a root, so today only an
      unlogged index reset to its init fork reaches bark_create_root_leaf,
      and an unlogged index writes no WAL; the record covers any logged
      index that is ever left without a root.

  XLOG_BARK_OVERWRITE (bark_overwrite_entry, from bark_coalesce_list)
      One leaf: an entry's offset and its replacement: a SINGLE or LIST
      re-encoded as a LIST or POSTING by bark_coalesce_list's general path.
      Primary and redo both call PageIndexTupleOverwrite, which keeps the
      offset and moves only the entries stored below the replaced one.  The
      record carries just the new entry, where a generic delta covered every
      byte that moved (pd_upper to the entry).

  XLOG_BARK_ADD_TID (bark_add_tid_entry, from bark_coalesce_list)
      One leaf: an entry's offset and one heap TID added to it, by either
      fast path (a LIST append, or an add to a POSTING set).  Like nbtree's
      INSERT_POST, the record carries only the TID: redo calls
      bark_entry_add_tid on the entry, as the primary did, and overwrites
      the entry with the result.  bark_entry_add_tid depends on nothing but
      the entry and the TID (it takes the key from the entry, not from the
      inserted tuple), so both sides build the same bytes;
      wal_consistency_checking checks that they do.

  XLOG_BARK_INSERT_SWAP (bark_swap_tid_entry, from bark_coalesce_list)
      One leaf: an entry's offset and a heap TID that falls inside the
      entry's TID range but does not fit in it (see "Heap TID order").  Like
      ADD_TID, it carries only the TID: redo divides the entry with
      bark_entry_swap_tid, as the primary did, and puts the two parts at the
      entry's offset and the next.  nbtree logs the same change as an
      INSERT_POST with a posting offset.  When the parts do not fit, the
      insert splits the page instead, and the SPLIT record carries them.

  XLOG_BARK_VACUUM (bark_delitems_vacuum)
      One leaf: the offsets of entries deleted whole, and the offsets and new
      contents of LIST and POSTING entries rewritten with the members that
      survive (nbtree logs which TIDs left a posting list instead and re-forms
      it in redo; re-forming a POSTING entry needs the tuple descriptor, which
      redo lacks).  Redo rewrites, then deletes, then clears the cycle ID.
      Redo takes a cleanup lock, as btree_xlog_vacuum does.  A page with
      nothing to delete gets a record only when it carries the running
      VACUUM's cycle ID, so a VACUUM that finds nothing logs nothing.

  XLOG_BARK_DELETE (bark_delitems_delete, from bark_bottomup_delete)
      One leaf, as nbtree's XLOG_BTREE_DELETE: the same payload as
      XLOG_BARK_VACUUM, plus the conflict horizon of the heap tuples whose
      entries go and whether the heap is a catalog for logical decoding.
      In hot standby, redo first resolves the snapshot conflict, then
      rewrites and deletes under an exclusive lock (see "Bottom-up
      deletion").  The page's vacuum cycle ID is left alone.

  XLOG_BARK_MERGE (bark_delitems_delete, from bark_merge_page)
      One leaf, as nbtree's XLOG_BTREE_DEDUP is a record of its own: the
      payload of XLOG_BARK_VACUUM, the offsets of the entries absorbed and
      the offsets and contents of the merged entries.  The changes are made
      in the other order, deletions first, since a merged entry is larger
      than the entry it replaces and fits on a full page only once the
      absorbed entries' space is free; the merged entries' offsets are
      numbered as after the deletions.  Redo takes an exclusive lock and
      shares bark_redo_delitems with VACUUM and DELETE.

  XLOG_BARK_UNLINK_PAGE (bark_delete_empty_leaf)
      Four pages: the target leaf (reinitialized as a deleted page from the
      record's links and safexid, no image needed), its left sibling's right
      link, its right sibling's left link, and the parent, where the target's
      downlink is pointed at the right sibling and the right sibling's own
      downlink is removed.  This is nbtree's MARK_PAGE_HALFDEAD and
      UNLINK_PAGE in one record, since BARK deletes only leaves whose
      right sibling has the same parent.  Redo takes exclusive locks on the
      left sibling, target, right sibling and parent, in that order, and
      holds them all until the record is applied.

  XLOG_BARK_OVERFLOW (bark_write_overflow_chain)
      Up to XLR_NORMAL_MAX_BLOCK_ID new overflow pages, each with its next
      link and its slice of the oversized tuple; redo rebuilds each page
      from those.  A chain longer than that takes several records, written
      before the entry that refers to the chain, so a crash in between
      leaves only unreferenced pages.

  XLOG_BARK_MARK_DELETED (bark_free_oversized)
      One overflow page, reinitialized as a deleted page that keeps its link
      to the next chunk.  Overflow pages are not in the tree, so the page has
      no siblings to fix, and UNLINK_PAGE's four-page layout would not fit.
      Redo takes an exclusive lock on the page.

  XLOG_BARK_REUSE_PAGE (bark_get_free_page)
      No pages.  The relation, block and the page's safexid as
      snapshotConflictHorizon, plus whether the heap is a catalog for
      logical decoding.  In hot standby, redo resolves the recovery conflict
      (see "Page deletion and recycling").  The page's reinitialization is in
      the allocating record that follows.

The vacuum cycle ID's clear is logged, in XLOG_BARK_VACUUM, and a split
logs the cycle ID it stamps, although nbtree treats the clear as a hint and
masks the field in btree_mask.  The clear could become a hint, with
bark_mask masking the field; logging it keeps every BARK page
byte-identical between primary and standby.  So bark_mask (wal_consistency_checking) masks only
the LSN, checksum, page-header hint bits and the unused space: BARK sets no
LP_DEAD bits and makes no other unlogged changes.

Entry encoding (the C-KEYSHAPE contract)
----------------------------------------

A BARK entry is a normal IndexTupleData: the key columns follow the tuple
header.  What varies is the value side.  BARK reuses PostgreSQL's per-AM
reserved bit, INDEX_AM_RESERVED_BIT in t_info, as an "alternate t_tid" marker
exactly as nbtree does.  When the bit is clear, t_tid is a plain heap locator.
When it is set, t_tid is repurposed: its offset-number field is split into a
high-nibble status region (BARK_STATUS_OFFSET_MASK) and a low twelve-bit count
(BARK_OFFSET_MASK), the same partition nbtree uses.

There are four entry shapes (BarkEntryShape):

  SINGLE   The plain nbtree shape.  The alt-TID bit is clear and t_tid holds a
           single heap locator.  A scalar unique or ordinary index produces
           only this shape.

  LIST     Sorted duplicates -- the libdb shape.  The alt-TID bit is set with
           BARK_IS_LIST; the locators are stored in ascending order in the
           tuple body after the key, and the low offset bits hold their count.
           Used when a key maps to a modest number of rows.

  POSTING  An inverted locator set.  The alt-TID bit is set with
           BARK_IS_POSTING; the set is stored as an sbm serialization (see
           lib/sbm.h) in the tuple body after the key: a uint16 holding the
           serialization's length, the serialization, and zero padding up to
           the entry's size.  The entry is sized for the set's removal bound
           (sbm_removal_bound), the largest serialization any subset of the
           set can have; see the invariants below.  Used when the set is large
           and clustered enough that sbm's compression wins even with that
           reserve, which costs at most one bit per locator of a dense run, so
           a scattered set stays a LIST.

  PIVOT    A downlink or high-key tuple on an internal page (or a leaf high
           key).  The alt-TID bit is set with neither list nor posting status;
           t_tid carries the child block number, and the low offset bits hold
           the number of key attributes actually present, since a pivot may be
           shortened by suffix truncation.  BARK_PIVOT_HEAP_TID says the
           pivot also has a heap TID, the last 6 bytes of its MAXALIGNed
           size (BarkPivotGetHeapTID).

  OVERSIZED  A key (or key + INCLUDE payload) whose formed tuple exceeds
           BarkMaxItemSize.  The alt-TID bit is set with BARK_IS_LIST and
           BARK_IS_POSTING together (a code point no other shape uses).  The
           full index tuple is stored out-of-line on a chain of BARK_OVERFLOW
           pages; the on-page entry is a bare header plus a BarkOverflowRef
           (full byte length, heap locator or downlink, and -- for a pivot --
           its key-attribute count and heap TID), with the first overflow
           block in t_tid.
           This is what lets BARK index a key larger than ~1/3 page, where
           nbtree errors: comparison, scan, uniqueness, and vacuum fetch the
           full value from the chain, so an oversized key behaves exactly like
           an inline key of the same value.  A normal-size entry never uses
           this shape, so it is pure overhead-free opt-in.

The encoding of a leaf entry is chosen per entry, by cardinality: a key with
one row is SINGLE, a few rows is LIST, and a large clustered set is POSTING.
This is what lets a scalar index and an inverted (GIN/RUM-style) index share
one access method and one page format, rather than needing an AM apiece.

Insert coalesces a row into an equal key's entry only in a non-unique index
whose equal keys always have equal images (bark_allequalimage, nbtree's
_bt_allequalimage rule: no INCLUDE columns, and every key opclass's
equalimage function says yes).  CREATE INDEX applies the same rule.  The
sort hands bark_load each run of equal keys with its heap TIDs ascending, as
nbtsort.c's _bt_load sees them when it deduplicates, and the run is written
as the smaller of LIST and POSTING for the longest prefix whose entry fits in
a tenth of a page, the limit _bt_load puts on a posting list, then the same
for the rest of the run.  Entries of BarkMaxItemSize would leave room for
only two of them on a leaf beside its high key.  Insert may later grow a
built entry up to BarkMaxItemSize.  The meta page's bark_allequalimage
records the rule's result, as nbtree's btm_allequalimage does; nothing reads
it yet.

Delete marking (the C-DELETE contract)
--------------------------------------

A leaf entry of any shape may be delete-marked: BARK_IS_DELETE_MARKED in the
status region records that the entry is logically deleted but retained so an
UNDO rollback can restore it, rather than being removed and left for VACUUM.
The bit value matches nbtree's BT_IS_DELETE_MARKED so the generic index
delete-marking path (index_delete_mark / index_undo_mark) treats both AMs
alike.  Delete-marking is orthogonal to an entry's shape and shares the status
region with it; it is never set on a PIVOT.

Invariants
----------

  * Block 0 is the meta page; its bark_magic is BARK_MAGIC and its
    bark_version is between BARK_MIN_VERSION and BARK_VERSION.
  * Keys are ordered by a btree-family operator class (BARK declares
    ambtreeopfamilies), so BARK's opfamily OIDs are btree sort opfamilies.
  * On a leaf page, entries are in key order; within one key, LIST locators
    are ascending and a POSTING set is canonical (see lib/sbm.h).  Entries of
    one key have disjoint TID ranges in ascending order, across pages too.
  * The alt-TID status bits are mutually exclusive in what they encode: an
    entry is exactly one of SINGLE, LIST, POSTING, OVERSIZED, or PIVOT;
    BARK_IS_DELETE_MARKED is an independent flag that may accompany a leaf
    shape but never a PIVOT, where the same bit is BARK_PIVOT_HEAP_TID.
  * An OVERSIZED entry's full tuple lives on a chain of BARK_OVERFLOW pages
    (bark_flags has BARK_OVERFLOW, not BARK_LEAF): each page holds up to
    BarkOverflowChunkSize raw bytes of the tuple and links to the next chunk
    via bark_next (BARK_P_NONE at the tail); the chunks concatenate to the full
    tuple.  Overflow pages carry no items and are skipped by the leaf scans
    (vacuum, cleanup) and by amcheck's per-page checks.
  * bark_page_id is BARK_PAGE_ID on every BARK page.
  * No leaf entry is larger than BarkMaxItemSize.
  * A high key is a pivot with between one and all of the index's key
    attributes, and no INCLUDE attributes, and a heap TID only with all of
    them.  Every key on its page is less than or equal to it, and strictly
    less when attributes were removed; a leaf entry equal to it is below its
    heap TID.  Every key on the right sibling is greater than or equal to it,
    by (key, lowest TID) on a leaf.
  * A downlink is a pivot with at most the index's key attributes, the first
    downlink of a level has none, and the first key on the child it points
    at is greater than or equal to it, by (key, lowest TID) on a leaf.
  * A POSTING entry is at least as large as BarkPostingEntrySize of its key
    and of sbm_removal_bound of its set.  An sbm is not size-monotone:
    removing members from a dense run turns all-ones vectors (a 2-bit flag)
    into stored mixed vectors and splits RLE runs into sparse chunks, so the
    survivors of a VACUUM can need more bytes than the whole set did.  The
    removal bound of a subset never exceeds that of the set, so when VACUUM
    re-encodes an entry's survivors (as a POSTING sized for their own bound, a
    LIST if that is smaller, or a SINGLE) the result fits the entry's slot,
    and VACUUM rewrites it in place in the page's one WAL record, never
    splitting a page or moving locators to another entry.  POSTING entries
    written before this reserve existed (with no length word) are not
    readable; BARK has not been released, so there is no version bump.
